const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How big should my concrete patio be?',
    a: 'Think about how you will use it. A table and four chairs needs roughly 12 by 12 feet, and a lounge area with a grill wants more. During the consultation we measure the yard, talk through how you use the space, and recommend a size and layout that fits both.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will a concrete patio crack in Toronto winters?',
    a: 'All concrete can develop hairline cracks, but the big failures come from poor prep. We compact a granular base, slope the patio to shed water away from the house, and place control joints so any shrinkage cracking happens where we planned it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What finish works best for a patio?',
    a: 'A broom or light texture finish gives the best footing when the surface is wet or icy. We can also add simple borders or a smoother trowel finish in seating areas. We will show you options during the quote visit.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long before I can use a new patio?',
    a: 'Foot traffic is fine after 24 to 48 hours. Give it about a week before heavy furniture and a full month before the heaviest loads, while the concrete reaches full strength.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you replace my old patio?',
    a: 'Yes. We remove the old surface, regrade and compact the base, and pour fresh concrete for a clean, modern look. Removal and disposal are included in your written quote.',
  },
];
